are of it."]},{"ply":71,"name":"36.Qb1","cls":"mistake","color":"w","headline":"36.Qb1 is a mistake: White's winning chances go from 62% to 52%.","points":["Black answers 36…Rxb7, which takes the rook on b7.","Better was 36.Ra7 (+1.36): the rook takes the open a-file."],"better":"36.Ra7 gets the rook out of the way of …Rxb7 and threatens Qa3, winning a knight for a pawn. After 36…Ra8 37.f5 Bxf5 38.Bxe7 Nxe7 39.Rxe7 Bxd3 40.Rd7, White is a pawn down (+1.36)."},{"ply":72,"name":"36…Rxb7","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"36…Rxb7! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Takes the rook on b7.","The next best move, 36…Rc8, would drop Black's winning chances from 48% to 11% (+5.55).","36…Rxb7 trades off the attacked rook instead of losing it to Rxb8.","White was threatening Rxb8, winning a rook. The rook takes something with it instead.","Trades rooks.","The rook on b7 can be taken by the queen on b1."]},{"ply":80,"name":"40…Nxf4","cls":"blunder","color":"b","headline":"40…Nxf4 is a blunder: Black's winning chances go from 44% to 12%.","points":["White answers 41.Nd6, which attacks the queen.","Better was 40…f5 (+0.63), which attacks the bishop on e4, which is undefended."],"better":"40…f5 wins a pawn, picking it up with 41…Nxf4, and threatens …fxe4, winning a bishop. Engine line: 41.Bf3 Nxf4 42.Nd6 Qd7 43.Qb8+ Kh7 44.Nxf5 Nxf5 45.Qxf4 Bd5 (+0.63)."},{"ply":81,"name":"41.Nd6","cls":"great","color":"w","headline":"41.Nd6! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Attacks the queen.","Moves the attacked knight to safety.","The next best move, 41.Bxe7, would drop White's winning chances from 88% to 65% (+1.74).","41.Nd6 wins a bishop, picking it up with 42.Nxe8, and threatens Nxe8, winning the queen.","Sets up a sequence and wins a bishop once the captures settle, picking it up with 42.Nxe8.","It threatens Nxe8, winning the queen, so Black has to spend the next move on it."]}],"opening":{"name":"English Opening: Agincourt Defense","eco":"A13","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 6.cxd5: 11 moves in all, 6 by White and 5 by Black.","Theory runs out after 6.cxd5: too few games between strong players reached this position to go further."]}}